{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Comfort, comfort my people.</b> This pericope is joined because above he mentioned that all the treasures of the king and also his sons will be exiled to Babylon; therefore, after this, the consolations:" "And these are the first consolations from the middle of the book according to the opinion of R. Moses the Kohen, concerning the Second Temple, but in my opinion it is all about our exile, only within the book there are words of the Babylonian exile for remembrance, for Cyrus who sent the exiles back, whereas at the end of the book the matters are for the future, as I shall explain. And know that the tradents of the commandments, of blessed memory, said that the book of Samuel, Samuel wrote, and it is true until 'and Samuel died' (1 Samuel 25:1). But behold, Chronicles proves that there it traces generation after generation of the sons of Zerubbabel, and the proof is 'kings shall see and arise, princes shall prostrate themselves' (Isaiah 49:7). One may answer this when they hear the prophet's name there, and if he is not there — and the discerning will understand:" "And the word <b>נחמו</b> is the speech of HaShem to His prophet or to the great ones of the people, and the reason for the doubling is for haste, or moment after moment:"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 40, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:03.237163270Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}